<title>USA: U.S. corn group praises EU gene corn decision.</title>
<headline>U.S. corn group praises EU gene corn decision.</headline>
<dateline>CHICAGO 1996-12-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The National Corn Growers Association Wednesday praised the European Union's decision to accept imports of genetically-modified corn.</p>
<p>&quot;We're pleased the EU came to the same conclusion that we and our top corn customers came to a long time ago,&quot; Wallie Hardie, president of the NCGA, said in a news release.</p>
<p>On Wednesday, the EU approved the market release of Bt corn, which is genetically altered to resist corn borer pests.</p>
<p>The biotechnology used to produce Bt corn will allow the U.S. to raise a healthier corn crop and will be good for the environment because the need for pesticides will be reduced, Hardie said.</p>
<p>Environmental groups in Europe and the U.S. have voiced concerns about introducing genetically-altered corn and soybeans into the food and feed chain.</p>
<p>Earlier this year, U.S. farmer delegates at the NCGA annual meeting adopted a policy that opposed any decision by a foreign country to prohibit or restrict the import of genetically modified farm products proven safe by the U.S. government, NCGA said.</p>
<p>((Chicago newsdesk 312-408-8720))</p>
